{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"cat collab/1793309600000-architect-yang-call-kimi-r4-arbitration.kimi-k3.1-pro.findings.md && sed -n '1,230p' collab/1793386000000-reviewer-adopt-fix-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md\"","aggregated_output":"NONECompleted-GMT: 2026-10-30 19:18:22 GMT\nCompleted-Local: 2026-10-31 02:18:22 +07\n\nConfirmation round against `abbfb998` (branch rebased onto `63674368`; everything re-read from `git diff 63674368..abbfb998`, not assumed from the pre-rebase review). I re-ran my own s1 probes for each arbitrated item, probed the eligibility follow-up, swept the new fix code adversarially, and re-ran the required suites. All probes ran read-only on the JVM lane through the test namespace's own runners (`run-outcome`, `walker-run`, `semantic-run`, `run-library`, and `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` for UAST-level construction); no source files edited, no commit made. Probe-honesty note: two of my early probe constructions were themselves broken (keyword vectors `[:go 3 0]` read as keyword calls, and `conj` on a quoted list reordering forms); the corrected runs below are the ones I stand behind, and one earlier \"crash\" (raw-entry application) was my harness passing forms to an AST runner — the corrected probe refuses cleanly.\n\n## Per-finding verification (s1 items, kimi r4 rulings applied)\n\n**P1-1 shadowable names — CLOSED.** Ruling (b): hygienic `dsg__` aliases, not binding rejection. Implemented as `dsg__get/dsg__equal/dsg__assoc/dsg__empty` bound around the whole program (clojure.cljc:1985-2040), referenced throughout `call-entry`/`admit-call-descriptor`/`compile-guest-call`; `dsg__` rejected in ANY user symbol position (check-program-symbols!, clojure.cljc:1873-1896; def names and binders separately guarded, 194-216). Probes (all fixed; each was a crash or corruption in s1): `(def f (let [get 5] (fn [x] x))) (f 1)` → `[:yin.k/ok 1]`; for each of `get = assoc empty?`: `(let [b 5] …fn…call)` → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`, `(def b 5)` then defn+call → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`, `(let [b not] …)` → `[:yin.k/ok 21]`; raise-path param shadows (`get`/`=`/`assoc`/`empty?` as the fn's parameter around a throwing callee) → `[:yin.k/error {:yin.k/message \"boom\", :yin.k/data {:x 21}}]` — the outcome survives intact.\n\n**P1-2 assoc-forged descriptors — CLOSED as probed; boundary re-opened at the protocol front door (N1 below).** Ruling (a): constructor/profile-produced descriptors distinguished from ordinary source maps. Admission witness: `admit-call-descriptor` (clojure.cljc:1072-1089) stores a closure captured over the canonical descriptor; `dsg__admitted?` reconstructs and compares via `data/content=` and never applies a source-supplied predicate; the gate wraps every source application (clojure.cljc:1239). Application-syntax probes: forged `(assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))` → `:yang.call/not-callable` (s1: `[:yin.k/ok 42]`); entry-swap f→g → refused; `(assoc f :extra :forged)` → refused; copy entry+admission keys → refused; `(apply h [21])` → refused; entry-spelled data with a non-callable entry stays data (`(yang.call/eligible? {:yang.call/entry 5})` → `[:yin.k/ok false]`). Negative fixtures exist (`test-common-call-source-admission`, 10 shapes × 2 wrappers). **But** the same forged map handed to `yang.call/invoke` runs — see N1.\n\n**P2-1 direct-apply branch — CLOSED.** Ruling (c): every source-resolved application through common admission; missing entry means `:yang.call/not-callable`. Probes: `(let [x 5] (x 1))` → `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/not-callable, :data {:yang.call/callee 5}}]` (s1: host crash); ordinary map and entry-less foreign value (`{:py/type :function :code :legacy}`) → same qualified condition; refusal also in tail position; non-symbol operators are gated too (`((if true f g) 21)` → 42 works, `((if false f 5) 21)` and `((yang.call/entry f) {})` → not-callable). Only generated `dsg__` implementation calls apply closures directly, as ruled.\n\n**P2-2 tail-chain stack growth — CLOSED.** Ruling (d): constant stack restored on BOTH lanes. Independent shapes (not the fixture's): 2-arg accumulator self-recursion `(go 1000 0)` → `[:yin.k/ok 1000]`, walker depth 1 at n=0 and n=1000, semantic depth 5 at both — constant; 3-cycle mutual chain (ma→mb→mc→ma, my shape) → `[:yin.k/ok :done]` both lanes, walker 1 / semantic 5, constant 0→1000; sensitivity control: non-tail `(grow n)` semantic depth 5 → 34 (n=10) → 304 (n=100) — the metric grows when it should (walker lane masks depth, as the fixture itself documents). The reinstated assertions (`d100 = d1000`, `≤ d0+2`, both lanes, non-tail negative control) are in `test-outcome-common-call-depth` (clojure_test.clj:1450-1484) and pass in the suite run below. Mechanism: outcome-token fast path in `call-entry` (clojure.cljc:1060-1069) and the tail `invoke-with` lane (1229-1238).\n\n**P3-1 continuation fallback — CLOSED.** Ruling (e): explicit refusal. Replacing `u/capture` with `:invalid-continuation` in compiled programs: operand position `(+ 1 (f 41))` and a non-tail fn body both → `[:yin.k/error {:yang.call/reason :yang.call/invalid-invocation, :data {:yang.call/reason :missing-raise-exit}}]` (clojure.cljc:1217-1227). Note the refusal only arms in non-tail bridges — tail calls use the token path — matching the fixture.\n\n**P3-2 named-argument classification — CLOSED (conformant per ruling (e); not re-raised, no new evidence).** Probe: `(yang.call/invoke f [] [[\"x\" 1]] raise)` → `[:yang.call/thrown \"Clojure functions do not accept named arguments\" [[\"x\" 1]]]` — reason, message and the named payload round-trip intact, as ruled.\n\n## Eligibility follow-up (abbfb998) — VERIFIED\n\n`(yang.call/eligible?)` as a source operator compiles to admitted-first-then-predicate (clojure.cljc:1286-1296); `fn?` in the guest library consults it (clojure.cljc:2146-2148). Probes via `#'yang.clojure/admit-call-descriptor` (the sanctioned profile construction path), both lanes: descriptor with `:yang.call/eligible?` → `true`: call works `[:yin.k/ok :called]`; predicate `false`: call refused `:yang.call/not-callable`; operator form honors the predicate (`true`/`false`); plain source fns still admitted (`[:yin.k/ok [true 42]]`). Matches `test-common-call-profile-eligibility`. Value-position references do NOT leak a structural answer: `(def p yang.call/eligible?) (p h)` → false and library `(fn? h)` → false for the forged `h` — consistent with the gated operator form (the library defn compiles through the same branch).\n\n## New findings (this round)\n\n**P2 | src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc:1299 (native-name? branch, via :588) + src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc:22,33-40 | Admission bypass at the protocol front door: `yang.call/invoke`/`invoke-with` skip the witness gate.** Namespaced operators are `native-name?`-true, so source calls to `yang.call/invoke` and `yang.call/invoke-with` compile through the direct native application branch and never pass `dsg__admitted?`; the runtime `yang.call/eligible?` they consult is structural **by design** (\"Descriptor projection remains structural after callable admission\", call.cljc:22). Probes: `(let [h (assoc {} :yang.call/entry (get f :yang.call/entry))] (yang.call/invoke h [21] [] raise))` → **`[:yin.k/ok 42]`** — the forged callee runs (same via `invoke-with`); control with the real `f` → 42, so the surface itself is legitimate and corpus-exercised. Sharper shapes: guest-fn or scalar entries forged the same way → raw host **\"Cannot apply non-function\"** — a defect, not a condition — the exact class ruling (c) said must not surface. Mitigations found: admitted-but-ineligible profiles still refuse through the runtime predicate hook, and the copied entry only re-runs its original body (no privilege escalation demonstrated — the forged callable is behaviorally the original). Still, ruling (a) states the boundary absolutely (\"Copying :yang.call/entry into it must not make it callable\"), and the copy IS callable here. Fix (either): extend the clojure.cljc:1286 compile-branch pattern to `invoke`/`invoke-with` so their callee operand is witness-gated (smallest change: one more branch case), or record with the architect that the protocol's own front door is the sanctioned profile-level construction channel, with a ledger row plus fixtures pinning exactly what it admits and that non-closure entries refuse with a qualified condition rather than a host defect.\n\n**P2 | docs/design/host-boundary/closure-{clj,cljs,cljd}.edn | Red gate test: the branch's new closure edge is not in the committed closure manifests.** `dao.host.gate-test/independent-traversal-agrees-with-the-committed-closure` fails on all three lanes: `walked, not committed #{src/cljc/yang/core/call.cljc}`. Cause: the adoption added yang.clojure's ns-level `(:require [yang.core.call :as call] …)` (clojure.cljc:28-30), so traversal B from `yin.repl.main` now reaches call.cljc on every lane, but no closure manifest lists it. Verified branch-caused: at `63674368` yang.clojure does not require yang.core.call (the only other requirer, the typescript prelude, is outside the closure). Outside the four required namespaces and the oracle (both green below) but a red test on the branch. Fix: regenerate the three manifests (traversal A lives in src/dev/host_boundary/closure.clj) in the adoption's follow-up; totals assertions in the same file will need the new counts.\n\n**P3 | informational | `fn?`/value-position predicates, `conj`/`vec` defs — probed, no action.** `(def conj 5)` / `(def vec 5)` are refused at compile (\"would capture the argument-vector constructor\") — the generated helpers' bare `conj` stays safe. `(fn? h)` and `(p h)` on forged `h` answer false consistently with the gated operator.\n\n## Independent suite re-verification (exact counts, JVM)\n\n- Four required (engineer brief): `yang.clojure-test` **85/763**, `yang.core.call-test` **4/13**, `yin.vm.macro-test` **25/188**, `yang.clojure.stream-eval-test` **13/309** — total **127 tests / 1273 assertions, 0 failures, 0 errors** (matches the fix2 report exactly; the known-gaps ledger's 126/1267 predates abbfb998's added regression, which reconciles the two).\n- Accumulated LaTTe oracle: `yang.latte-oracle-test` **21 tests / 971 assertions, 0 failures**.\n- Bonus regression: `yang.python.antlr.call-entry-test` **1/4, 0 failures**.\n- Kondo could not be independently re-run in this sandbox (`clojure -M:kondo` approval denied); the engineer's 0-errors/2-pre-existing-oracle-warnings claim stands unverified by me.\n- Broader JVM suite (accidental full run via `-M:test`): reached dao.host.gate-test with only the N2 three-lane failure before the run was cut at its time limit; everything before it green.\n\n## CLOSED/OPEN table\n\n| Item | Ruling | Verdict |\n|---|---|---|\n| P1-1 shadowable names capture generated code | (b) dsg__ aliases | **CLOSED** (all 17 probes) |\n| P1-2 assoc-forged descriptors | (a) admission witness | **CLOSED as probed** — boundary re-opened at `yang.call/invoke` (new P2, N1) |\n| P2-1 direct-apply too broad | (c) admission for source calls | **CLOSED** (qualified refusal everywhere probed, incl. non-symbol operators) |\n| P2-2 tail-chain stack growth | (d) constant stack both lanes | **CLOSED** (independent shapes + reinstated assertions green) |\n| P3-1 continuation fallback | (e) explicit refusal | **CLOSED** (`:yang.call/invalid-invocation` + `:missing-raise-exit`) |\n| P3-2 named-arg classification | (e) conformant, not re-raised | **CLOSED (conformant)** — payload round-trip intact |\n| Eligibility follow-up (abbfb998) | abbfb998 | **VERIFIED** (predicate gates calls both lanes; plain fns unaffected) |\n| N1 invoke/invoke-with witness bypass | new, needs architect | **OPEN** (P2) |\n| N2 closure manifests missing call.cljc | new, repo hygiene | **OPEN** (P2; red gate test, all 3 lanes) |\n\n## Sign-off readiness\n\n**Not ready yet — two P2s from this round, both small and well-bounded.** All six arbitrated items are closed with probe evidence and the four required namespaces + the oracle corpus are green at exactly the reported counts, so the r4 fix work itself is confirmed. Before Architect sign-off: (1) N1 needs an architect decision — gate `yang.call/invoke`/`invoke-with` callees at the compile branch like `yang.call/eligible?`, or record the front door as the sanctioned construction channel with a ledger row and refusal fixtures (including qualified refusal, not a host crash, for non-closure forged entries); (2) N2 needs the three closure manifests regenerated. If the architect accepts N1 as a recorded deviation with fixtures, the round can proceed to sign-off with N2's manifest regeneration as the only code-side touch.\n\n---\n\nExecution summary: probes A1–A6, B, C1–C4 ran via one-line `clojure -M -e` against the loaded test namespace; suites ran via `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. One background full-suite run (accidental, via `-M:test -e`) was cut at its time limit after surfacing the N2 gate failures — reported as observed, not completed. No files in the repo were modified and nothing was committed; the report above is the deliverable since collab/ writes were denied.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '560,605p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1130,1175p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1260,1338p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1860,1957p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,110p' docs/agents/build-n-test.md && sed -n '560,605p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1130,1175p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1260,1338p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc && sed -n '1860,1957p' src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"---\ndescription: Build, lint, test commands, TDD guidelines, and bracket debugging for Clojure, ClojureScript, Node.js, and ClojureDart\n---\n\n# BUILD & TEST\n\n## Commands\n\n```sh\n# Tests (Babashka runner)\nbb test              # All three lanes (JVM, Node, Dart), fast default form: no slow tests\nbb test:all          # Every test on every lane, slow ones included (JVM takes 20+ min)\nbb test:slow         # Only the slow tests, on every lane\n# One lane alone: <task>:clj, <task>:cljs, <task>:cljd\nbb test:clj          # JVM, fast: skips ^:slow (bare `clj -M:test` runs them too)\nbb test:cljs         # Node (shadow-cljs :cljs alias), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:cljd         # Dart (needs flutter on PATH), fast: guarded slow bodies print SKIP\nbb test:all:clj      # JVM, no filter: `clojure -M:test`\nbb test:all:cljs     # Node with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:all:cljd     # Dart with DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:clj     # JVM: `-i :slow` (long-loops-test, ~14 min)\nbb test:slow:cljs    # Node: only the namespaces that use dao.test-slow/guard, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\nbb test:slow:cljd    # Dart: only those namespaces' generated tests, DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1\n# Slow tests: tag a deftest ^:slow. The JVM runner then excludes it from `bb test:clj`.\n# Node and Dart have no tag filter, so a .cljc slow test also wraps its body in\n# (dao.test-slow/guard \"name\" (fn [] ...)); it prints SKIP unless DATOM_SLOW_TESTS=1.\n# Node and Dart select slow tests by NAMESPACE (derived by grepping `slow/guard`\n# under test/), so the non-slow tests inside those namespaces also run there.\n# On Node, shadow also runs the test namespaces those nine require (about\n# seven more, e.g. lower-portable-test); on Dart only the nine namespaces run.\n# Run test:slow / test:all before a big merge or a commit that touches many parts.\n# Run one Dart lane at a time per machine (the limit is cores and the Flutter cache,\n# not subsystems). On the many-core remote hosts see \"Remote test servers\" below for\n# which lanes may overlap.\n#\n# When to tag a test slow: when it takes more than about 5 s on any lane (the\n# Python e2e tests sit on a ~3 s floor, so their JVM cut is 3.0 s). The\n# default lanes must stay in minutes; 22 JVM tests (0.75% of the suite) were\n# 81% of its test time. Tag it ^:slow on the JVM; if it is a .cljc test, also\n# wrap its body in dao.test-slow/guard so Node and Dart skip it by default. A\n# test that is slow only on Dart (dao.jing.dht-test/unproven-chunks-... takes\n# 72 s there, 3.6 s on the JVM) is a candidate for a speed investigation first.\n# Python slow set (2026-10-06): 65 ^:slow and 15 guard-only tests in the\n# yang.python.antlr e2e, e2e-c1, prelude-parity, safepoint, float-address\n# and int-contract tests; a Python slice landing runs `bb test:sub:clj yang.python --slow`\n# (or `clojure -M:test -i :slow -n <ns>`).\n# While iterating, use the Gate Ladder (see below): G0 iterate via `bb test:sub:clj <own>`\n# or `clojure -M:test -n <ns>`. Do not run the full `bb test` while iterating.\n# At slice checkpoints, run G1 (`bb test:sub <own>`). Before landing a slice, run\n# G2 (`bb test:changed` in the worktree across all three lanes).\n# Full repo-wide `bb test` (G3) is the mandatory landing gate on master after merge.\n# Cross-host bugs show only on Node or Dart, so tri-host verification is mandatory\n# for every gate from G1 upward. Run one lane set at a time per machine to avoid\n# contention (a laptop has 8 cores; the 24- and 48-core remote hosts have room to\n# overlap lanes, see \"Remote test servers\" below).\n# Measured fast-lane times (2026-10-03): bb test about 14 min = JVM 5.5 + Node\n# 3.8 + Dart 4.3, plus builds. test:slow: Node and Dart about 5 min each (mostly\n# compile); the JVM half is estimated at 17 min (long-loops-test alone is 14),\n# not yet run end to end. test:all: JVM 20+ min, also an estimate.\nnpm test             # Node.js tests\n\n# Cross-host peers the JVM lane spawns. `bb test` builds both. A bare\n# `bb test:clj` builds only the Node REPL (it depends on build:yin-repl-node);\n# without build:yin-repl-peer first, the Dart-peer tests skip with a printed notice.\n# Under `bb test:sub` and `bb test:changed`, prerequisites are derived automatically.\nbb build:yin-repl-peer   # Dart exe build/yin-repl-peer (yin.repl R5 pairs)\nbb build:yin-repl-node   # Node REPL target/yin-repl.js; REQUIRED by\n                         # yin.repl.dht-process-test (JVM-to-Node DHT reader,\n                         # and the linker L5 Node reader that requires a module\n                         # by name): `clojure -M:test` without it fails that test\n```\n\n### Changed-only tests: `bb test:changed`\n\n`bb test:changed` (src/dev/affected.clj) runs the fast tests a change can\nreach: the reverse require closure of the files changed since the\nmerge-base with master, plus fixtures that mention a changed resource path,\ntests that walk a changed tree, and each src file's `_test` by convention.\nA walker is a file calling file-seq, fs/glob, fs/list-dir,\nfs/walk-file-tree or .listFiles; each string literal argument of its\nio/file, fs/file, fs/path, fs/glob, java.io.File. or File. calls is a root\n(let-bound roots count), and a change under a root selects it. The three\nlanes run in parallel (logs: target/profile/changed-<lane>.log) after the\nneeded builds. `test:changed:list` shows what and why; `:clj`/`:cljs`/\n`:cljd` run one lane; `--base REF`; `--changed FILE...` (absolute OK; a\ntypo, an empty list or the wrong cwd exits 1). Use it to iterate and to\nland a slice confined to a package. Exit 2 (from :list too) is a wide\nchange (deps.edn bb.edn shadow-cljs.edn mise.toml .cljstyle src/dev/\nantlr/ .clj-kondo/ package*/pubspec*): it runs nothing; run `bb test`,\nas for a change to the core everything requires (yin.vm engine,\ndao.stream, codecs) and before a big merge. Not seen: dynamic loads\n(`requiring-resolve`, `resolve`), paths or walk roots built at run time,\nand spawned programs that changed (target/yin-repl.js, Dart peers).\n\n### Sharded quick JVM feedback and change routing\n\n```sh\nbb test:quick-sharded                           # Full G3-sized JVM quick lane, 4 workers\nbb test:quick-sharded --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:quick-sharded --base master --workers 6  # Changed selection, explicit worker limit\nbb test:quick-sharded --list --changed src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc\nbb test:routed docs/agents/build-n-test.md       # Explicit changed paths (absolute OK)\nbb test:routed --dry-run deps.edn               # Print dispatch; executes no gate\nbb test:routed                                 # Git change set, same base as test:changed\nbb deps:npm                                    # Content-checked local npm ci\nbb -cp src/dev -m quick-sharded-test            # Runner/routing/cache self-tests\n```\n\n`test:quick-sharded` is a JVM-only execution alternative for broad G2 and\nthe JVM portion of G3. Without selection flags it selects every JVM test;\n;;           (let [a (data/nth dsg__av 0)] e1)\n;;           (if (data/content= (data/count dsg__av) 2)\n;;             (let [a (data/nth dsg__av 0) b (data/nth dsg__av 1)] e2)\n;;             (data/count))))                     ; refused: no such arity\n;;\n;; The dispatch names are module-qualified, which no let binder can shadow.\n;; A call is (f (conj [] a b)) in tail position when the operator is not\n;; statically native; a native name (a primitive or module export, not bound\n;; locally and not defined by the program) is called directly, and in value\n;; position becomes a fresh adapter closure over the same convention. The\n;; program's own macro names stay ordinary applications: the expander\n;; recognizes those calls, so their operands must stay ordinary.\n\n(def ^:private av-sym (symbol (str reserved-prefix \"av\")))\n\n\n(def ^:private default-adapter-arity\n  \"The largest count an adapter admits for a native name with no declared\n   profile (`:native` given as a plain set).\"\n  4)\n\n\n(def ^:private adapter-variadic-cap\n  \"How far an adapter extends a `:variadic` profile arity: an adapter is a\n   finite dispatch, so an open-ended arity needs a stated bound.\"\n  8)\n\n\n(defn- native-name?\n  \"True when `sym` statically names a primitive or host-module export.\"\n  [env sym]\n  (when-let [conv (::convention env)]\n    (and (symbol? sym)\n         (not (contains? (locals env) sym))\n         (not (contains? (::defined env) sym))\n         (or (some? (namespace sym))\n             (contains? (:native conv) sym)))))\n\n\n(defn- macro-call?\n  [env macro-env sym]\n  (and (symbol? sym)\n       (not (contains? (locals env) sym))\n       (or (contains? macro-env sym)\n           (contains? (:macros (::convention env)) sym))))\n\n\n    :else (reject! \"fn parameters must be a vector\" {:params (first operands)})))\n\n\n(defn- adapter-counts\n  \"The argument counts an adapter for native `sym` admits: the fixed arities\n   of its `:yin.k/arities` profile, extended to `adapter-variadic-cap` when\n   the profile is `:variadic`. A name with no declared profile admits\n   0 to `default-adapter-arity`.\"\n  [env sym]\n  (if-let [declared (get-in env [::convention :arities sym])]\n    (let [fixed (filter number? declared)\n          from (if (seq fixed) (apply max fixed) 0)]\n      (vec (sort (distinct (concat fixed\n                                   (when (some #{:variadic} declared)\n                                     (range from (inc (max from adapter-variadic-cap)))))))))\n    (vec (range (inc default-adapter-arity)))))\n\n\n(defn- compile-adapter\n  \"A fresh closure over the convention that calls native `sym` with the\n   unpacked arguments, for each admitted count.\"\n  [sym env tail? macro-env]\n  (let [arities (mapv (fn [n]\n                        (let [call (cons sym (map #(list 'data/nth av-sym %) (range n)))]\n                          {:n n\n                           :form (if (outcomes? env)\n                                   (lower-function-body env macro-env [] [call])\n                                   call)}))\n                      (adapter-counts env sym))]\n    (compile-lambda [av-sym] (av-dispatch env sym nil arities) env tail? macro-env)))\n\n\n(defn- apply-call?\n  \"True when `operator` is the `apply` intrinsic: not bound locally and not\n   defined by the program.\"\n  [env operator]\n  (and (= 'apply operator)\n       (not (contains? (locals env) operator))\n       (not (contains? (::defined env) operator))))\n\n\n(defn- compile-guest-call\n  \"Admit a source operator before common invocation. Only generated UAST\n   implementation calls apply private closures directly.\"\n  [callee-ast arguments-ast env tail?]\n                                        coll-form)\n                                      false env macro-env)\n                        env tail?)))\n\n\n(defn- compile-av-application\n  [operator operands env tail? macro-env]\n  (cond\n    (apply-call? env operator) (compile-apply operands env tail? macro-env)\n\n    ;; (:k m) / (:k m default): a static lookup\n    (keyword? operator)\n    (do (when-not (<= 1 (count operands) 2)\n          (reject! \"A keyword call takes a map and an optional default\"\n                   {:keyword operator, :operands (count operands)}))\n        (when (contains? (locals env) 'get)\n          (reject! \"A local binding of get would capture a keyword lookup\"\n                   {:keyword operator}))\n        (cond-> {:type :application\n                 :operator {:type :variable, :name 'get}\n                 :operands (into [(compile-form (first operands) false env macro-env)\n                                  (compile-literal operator false)]\n                                 (map #(compile-form % false env macro-env))\n                                 (rest operands))}\n          tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\n\n    (and (outcomes? env) (= operator 'yang.call/eligible?))\n    (let [candidate 'dsg__eligibility_candidate]\n      (cond-> (u/mark-tails\n                (apply u/app\n                       (u/lam [candidate]\n                              (u/if-node\n                                (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v candidate))\n                                (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/eligible?) (u/v candidate))\n                                (u/lit false)))\n                       (map #(compile-form % false env macro-env) operands)))\n        tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\n\n    (and (outcomes? env)\n         (#{'yang.call/invoke 'yang.call/invoke-with} operator)\n         (<= 4 (count operands)))\n    (let [candidate 'dsg__invocation_candidate\n          compile-operand #(compile-form % false env macro-env)]\n      (cond-> (u/mark-tails\n                (u/app\n                  (u/lam [candidate]\n                         (u/if-node\n                           (u/app (u/v 'dsg__admitted?) (u/v candidate))\n                           (apply u/app (u/v operator) (u/v candidate)\n                                  (map compile-operand (rest operands)))\n                           (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/transfer)\n                                  (compile-operand (nth operands 3))\n                                  (u/app (u/v 'yang.call/condition)\n                                         (u/lit :yang.call/not-callable)\n                                         (u/app (u/v 'dsg__assoc) (u/lit {})\n                                                (u/lit :yang.call/callee)\n                                                (u/v candidate))))))\n                  (compile-operand (first operands))))\n        tail? (assoc :tail? true)))\n\n    ;; a statically native name: call it directly\n    (native-name? env operator)\n    (cond-> {:type :application\n             :operator (cond-> {:type :variable, :name operator}\n                         (seq (meta operator)) (with-meta (meta operator)))\n             :operands (mapv #(compile-form % false env macro-env) operands)}\n      tail? (assoc :tail? true))\n\n    :else\n    (compile-guest-call (compile-form operator false env macro-env)\n                        (compile-form (vec operands) false env macro-env)\n                        env tail?)))\n\n\n(defn compile-application\n  \"Compile a function application to Universal AST.\n\n  Clojure: (+ 1 2)\n  AST: {:type :application\n   declares domain failures to the host adapter returning its outcome.\"\n  [forms opts]\n  (if-not opts\n    {}\n    (do (when-not (= :arg-vector (:convention opts))\n          (reject! \"Unknown compile-program convention\" {:opts opts}))\n        (when-let [hit (seq (filter control-heads (:macros opts)))]\n          (reject! \"A :macros entry names a compiler form\" {:macros (vec hit)}))\n        (let [defined (into #{}\n                            (keep (fn [f]\n                                    (when (and (seq? f)\n                                               (contains? #{'def 'defn 'defn- 'defmacro} (first f))\n                                               (symbol? (second f)))\n                                      (strip-symbol-meta (second f)))))\n                            forms)]\n          ;; the compiler emits bare conj and vec (collection literals, apply),\n          ;; so a program definition of either would capture them\n          (when-let [hit (first (filter #{'conj 'vec} defined))]\n            (reject! (str \"Defining \" hit \" would capture the argument-vector constructor\")\n                     {:name hit}))\n          (cond-> {::convention {:native (set (if (map? (:native opts))\n                                                (keys (:native opts))\n                                                (:native opts)))\n                                 :arities (into {}\n                                                (keep (fn [[sym profile]]\n                                                        (when-let [a (if (map? profile)\n                                                                       (:yin.k/arities profile)\n                                                                       profile)]\n                                                          [sym a])))\n                                                (when (map? (:native opts)) (:native opts)))\n                                 :macros (set (:macros opts))}\n                   ::defined defined}\n            (:outcomes opts) (assoc ::outcomes {:adapters (or (:adapters opts) {})}))))))\n\n\n(defn- check-program-symbols!\n  \"The program pre-pass. Quoted data is exempt. The reserved prefix names\n   compiler temporaries, so no user symbol may carry it in ANY position\n   (binder, reference, definition name); `loop` and `recur` are special forms,\n   so they may only head a list, never stand as a value or a binder.\"\n  [form]\n  (cond\n    (symbol? form)\n    (do (when (reserved-name? form)\n          (reject! \"Symbol uses the reserved destructuring prefix\"\n                   {:symbol form, :prefix reserved-prefix}))\n        (when (and (nil? (namespace form)) (contains? #{'loop 'recur} form))\n          (reject! (str form \" is only legal in operator position\") {:symbol form})))\n    (seq? form)\n    (when-not (= 'quote (first form))\n      (let [head (first form)]\n        (when (and (symbol? head) (reserved-name? head))\n          (reject! \"Symbol uses the reserved destructuring prefix\"\n                   {:symbol head, :prefix reserved-prefix}))\n        (when-not (symbol? head)\n          (check-program-symbols! head))\n        (run! check-program-symbols! (rest form))))\n    (map?